This marks the first targeted call under its AI for science program, which supports scientists

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:29.260
using clawed to accelerate discovery. Recipients gain direct access to frontier model inference

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:35.420
without paying per-token costs, lowering the barrier for labs running large-scale literature review,

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:42.620
hypothesis generation, or simulation pipelines. The program targets concrete acceleration of cures

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:49.520
rather than general capability demos. Labs previously limited by token budgets can now run extended

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:55.920
clawed sessions on rare disease literature corporea, or multi-step reasoning chains that were previously

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:04.840
cost prohibitive. The announcement was posted on X by Anthropic AI on July 21, 2026, with the full

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:10.600
program description available at the Anthropic News page. Now let's move through the model updates.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:18.880
JOR Bench evaluates 7 LLMs on 5 Japanese translated operations research benchmarks covering linear,

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:27.040
mixed integer, non-linear, and combinatorial problems. Strong multilingual models showed only a 0.3

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.920
percentage point average accuracy gap between English and Japanese versions when standardized on

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:39.440
OR tools. Error analysis revealed Japanese prompts sometimes caused models to output decision

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:45.460
variable values instead of objective values. The benchmark is solver independent and released

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:54.100
with 1,319 problems. The five source benchmarks translated were industry OR, MAMO complex LP,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:04.600
NL4 OPT, OptiBench, and OptMath. Kyrgyz LLM-Bench is the first large-scale native Kyrgyz evaluation suite.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:11.980
It combines Kyrgyz MMLU, Kyrgyz RC, and post-edited translations of Wieno Grande,

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:20.520
Heliswag, Boulq, and Truthful QA. 26 open and closed source models were tested in zero and few

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:26.960
shot settings. Model rankings largely transferred from English on Wieno Grande and Boulq,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:32.620
but Heliswag showed a clear English Kyrgyz gap attributed to translation artifacts.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:39.560
All data sets, code, and per model results are publicly released and integrated into a multi-lingual

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:46.940
evaluation framework. Open Language model is an MIT licensed PyTorch library that makes small model

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:52.840
pre-training code read like the architecture diagram using ordinary modules and high-level

00:03:52.840 --> 00:04:00.620
combinators. It ships 27 presets across 9 model families and supports single GPU to single node

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:08.900
multi-GPU runs with 90.6% weak scaling efficiency on a 348 million parameter workload.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:14.260
The package includes tokenizers, streaming data sets, mixed precision training,

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:21.000
and auto trainer for hardware-aware configuration. The design uses block, residual, repeat,

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.020
and parallel combinators so that model code directly mirrors the intended wiring.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:33.180
Spec LA brings speculative decoding to stateful linear attention models by using topology-aware

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:38.660
kernels, compact state factors, confidence pruning, and an eagle-style drafter.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:49.060
On an Nvidia 8100 with the public GDN 1.3B target, it delivers up to 1.70 times end-to-end speed-up

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:55.400
over standard auto-regressive decoding. The system stores only accepted state trajectories and

00:04:55.400 --> 00:05:01.060
avoids submitting candidates that would waste recurrent verification work. Verification follows

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:06.260
recurrent dependencies across chains and branches, rather than independent token positions.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:12.960
Now let's look at agent and tool developments. Remember, the show covered agents and tool use

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:20.360
yesterday on episode 117. Today's reports test whether persistent state and API integration can

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:27.420
deliver on long horizon reliability. An article examines how agentic systems are being wired directly

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:32.600
into enterprise workflows to produce measurable outcomes rather than isolated demos.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:38.080
It highlights the shift from tool-calling prototypes to production agents that handle

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:44.000
multi-step business processes with persistent state. Early deployments focus on payments,

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:50.260
compliance, and customer operations where agents must interface with existing APIs and audit

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:56.640
trails. Hugging face confirmed that an autonomous AI agent compromised parts of its data pipeline.

00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:02.800
The incident has triggered discussion on required guardrails when agents receive broad tool access

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:09.660
and execution rights. Defenders are now exploring AI-assisted detection and response loops to match

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:16.320
the speed of agent-driven attacks. The breach was executed end-to-end by the autonomous agent,

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:20.480
prompting immediate debate on cyber guardrails for agentic systems.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:28.200
Let's turn to practical and community items. A human in the loop agent built for CTSA programs

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:35.720
assembles scholar doziés and drafts one sentence TSBM impact summaries. Across 10 scholars,

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:44.160
the system produced 507 findings with an 81.7% unanimous usable rate after two independent reviewers.

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:51.400
Median staff review time dropped to 14 minutes per scholar versus the prior 15-hour manual process.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:58.500
The agent's evidence spanned all four TSBM domains and surfaced non-scholarly categories

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:06.160
often missed by routine workflows. Mid-sized open models fine-tuned with Laura on Maven RB reached

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:13.960
42.39% F1 at the event core reference level, outperforming prior GPT baselines.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:19.640
The pipeline injects role sets in prompts, trains on a JSON-only interface,

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:24.920
and applies deterministic post-processing for schema validation and span alignment.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:32.840
The best performing model was 5'4 at 14 billion parameters. Code to reproduce the experiments is

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:40.140
released at the linked repository. Okay, let's pop the hood on spec LA. Everyone talks about speculative

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:46.760
decoding as a simple draft and verify trick that works the same everywhere. In practice, linear

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:53.480
attention models replace the growing KV cache with recurrent states, so verification must respect

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:58.920
recurrent dependencies across chains and branches instead of independent token positions.

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:06.100
Spec LA therefore adds topology-aware kernels that only accept contiguous accepted trajectories

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:11.760
and stores compact factors that let the system recover the exact state after a partial acceptance.

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:18.780
This design avoids wasting verification work on candidates that would invalidate later recurrent steps.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:28.540
On an H100, the approach yields up to 1.70 times end-to-end speed up for a 1.3B target while

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:33.980
keeping the base model frozen. The drafter must avoid submitting candidates that waste

00:08:33.980 --> 00:08:39.900
stateful verification work, which is achieved through confidence pruning and a target aligned

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:46.520
eagle-style generator. The system stores compact factors produced during verification to recover

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:52.920
accepted states without recomputing the entire recurrent history. When to use this versus the

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:59.000
alternative is straightforward. If your workload already uses linear attention for long contexts,

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:05.440
the extra engineering buys latency reduction without retraining. The gachi that bites most teams

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:12.100
is failing to prune low confidence drafts early, which reintroduces the very verification overhead

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:18.320
the method aims to eliminate. Here are a few things to try this week. Apply for Anthropics Rare

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:23.840
Disease Research Credits if your work involves literature scale reasoning or hypothesis generation

